# KNOWN GAP (SELF-HOST lane) — the self-host cannot compile ANY method call on
# a `Box[UserStruct]`. It mangles `Box__<Inner>__<method>` and emits a call to a
# symbol nothing defines, so the link fails:
#
#     undefined reference to `Box__BareBox__plain_method'
#
# Rust gg auto-derefs the `Box` to the inner type and dispatches the inherent
# `equip` method; the self-host does not.
#
# ⚠ THE GAP IS NOT ABOUT THE FOUR TAG-CHECK NAMES, and `plain_method` is the
# cell that proves it: an ordinarily-named method fails exactly the same way.
# The `is_ok` cell is here only because this is where the `t1017` provenance
# audit found the gap — `Box[UserStruct]` is one of the receiver provenances
# that fix covers on the Rust lane, and it is the ONE cell of
# `user_four_name_method_provenance.gg`'s axis that could not join that fixture,
# because a top-level fixture is auto-enrolled into `runtime_parity_corpus` and
# a self-host non-MATCH there would be the round's own inflow.
#
# `Box[Trait]` (the trait-object form) is a DIFFERENT path and works on both
# lanes — it dispatches through the vtable, not through a mangled inner name.
# That cell IS in `user_four_name_method_provenance.gg` and is green.
#
# Filed as `todo/t1020`. Measured 2026-09-03 with the self-host lowerer driver;
# Rust gg builds and prints `true` / `true` for the same program.
#
# INTENDED (self-host lane):
#   true
#   true
struct BareBox:
    int v

equip BareBox:
    bool plain_method(self):
        return self.v > 0

    bool is_ok(self):
        return self.v > 0

void main():
    Box[BareBox] u = Box.new(BareBox(5))
    print(u.plain_method())
    print(u.is_ok())
